### The Idea

I want to build an international organization and media community around **high-achieving teenagers who are extraordinarily good at something—but are still teenagers.**

It would begin with the world of academic competitions: IMO, IOI, IPhO, IChO, IBO, and other olympiads. The competitions bring these students together, but the project would focus on everything beyond the scoreboard: their personalities, friendships, humor, cultures, hobbies, failures, weird interests, and the ways they actually experience achievement.

The inspiration is partly K-pop. Groups have a shared craft, but people become attached to the *people*: the inside jokes, collaborations, friendships between groups, chaotic interviews, behind-the-scenes moments, and personalities. I want to create something with that same sense of **community and lore**, but for young people who compete at an extremely high level academically.

The goal would not be to make another polished “genius teenager” highlight reel.

It would be:

> **Look at these teenagers. They happen to be extraordinarily good at something.**

Maybe you spend five minutes talking about an IMO problem, and then twenty minutes arguing about ping-pong. Maybe someone is a world-class mathematician and also plays viola, loves some completely random hobby, tells terrible jokes, or is nervous about talking to people. Maybe an IMO kid collaborates with an IOI kid, and eventually students from different olympiads start creating things together.

The competition is the common language. **The people are the subject.**

### Why I Care About It

This connects directly to my research on achievement and the mental models surrounding high school success. So much of what we see—especially through social media—turns achievement into a polished image: perfect grades, medals, productivity, genius, success.

I want to show the reality underneath that image.

What does it actually feel like to be a teenager who is extremely good at something?

What happens to your identity when everyone around you is also exceptional?

What do these students care about outside their achievement?

What do they struggle with?

What makes them laugh?

What does their relationship with their craft actually look like?

I want to make high achievement feel **human rather than mythical.**

### Why It Is International

Eventually, I don't want this to belong to one country.

The beauty of the olympiad world is that teenagers from all over the world can meet because of something they love. They arrive representing different countries, but for a while they are simply a bunch of teenagers who understand the same strange thing.

The project would try to capture that.

**It starts with one field. It becomes a network. The network becomes international.**

And perhaps students from different olympiads eventually start their own versions, so it grows beyond mathematics.

### The Long-Term Vision

This could eventually become an actual organization—not just a channel.

It could include:

* long-form interviews and conversations
* behind-the-scenes coverage of competitions
* collaborations between olympiad communities
* international student-created content
* stories about failure, growth, identity, and life beyond achievement
* humorous/chaotic content that lets people actually know the students
* eventually, a network where high-achieving students themselves help shape and create the culture

The project would be ambitious and would require significant trust, authorization, safeguarding, organization, and eventually cooperation with international competition communities.

So I can't build the whole thing now.

And that's part of what makes it meaningful.

### The Path to It

To build this properly, I first have to **enter the world myself.**

I need to become good at the things I care about. I need to experience the competitions. I need to meet the people. I need to understand the culture from the inside. I need to develop the communication, research, and organizational skills to eventually build something larger.

So my current work isn't separate from the project.

**It is part of becoming capable of building it.**

I'm not working toward this because I need to prove that I'm worthy of being there.

I'm working because **there is a world I want to encounter—and eventually, I want to contribute something to it.**

### The Core Idea

**Achievement doesn't erase the teenager.**

I want to build a world where extraordinarily accomplished young people can be seen not only as medalists, programmers, mathematicians, scientists, or representatives of their countries, but as whole human beings.

A world where the brilliant kids are allowed to be weird.

Where they can be serious and ridiculous.

Where they can compete and collaborate.

Where they can be excellent without having to become perfect.

Where the scoreboard is only one small part of the story.

**Gremlins Who Occasionally Produce Clever Mathematics.**

Maybe that's where it starts.
